steps {
  type: CORTEX_STEP_TYPE_USER_INPUT
  status: CORTEX_STEP_STATUS_DONE
  metadata {
    created_at {
      seconds: 1786379241
      nanos: 41311835
    }
    source: CORTEX_STEP_SOURCE_USER_EXPLICIT
  }
  user_input {
    user_response: "# AI Studio Prompt \342\200\224 Jokdel Royal Form Builder + Simple CRM\n\nPaste this into AI Studio. No images are required for this build, but if you want the public form pages to visually match the main site, you can attach the Home page PNG from earlier for reference.\n\n---\n\nBuild a simple two-part web app for a Nigerian real estate company called **Jokdel Royal** (sales of land & houses, letting/rentals as an intermediary, and property management):\n\n1. A **public-facing form site** where prospective clients fill out inquiry forms.\n2. An **admin panel** where staff can build/edit those forms, view submitted responses, and manage them like a lightweight CRM.\n\nKeep the overall interface **very simple and uncluttered** \342\200\224 this is a lightweight internal tool, not a marketing site. Prioritize clarity and usability over decoration.\n\n## Brand Styling\n- Primary background: white (#FFFFFF).\n- Brand dark navy: #131B2E \342\200\224 headers, nav, primary buttons, form field focus states, admin sidebar background.\n- Accent wine/burgundy: #6E1E1E \342\200\224 used sparingly for status tags, alerts, or \"new\" indicators.\n- Typography: serif (Playfair Display or equivalent) for page titles/headings only; clean sans-serif (Inter or equivalent) for all form labels, inputs, buttons, tables, and body text \342\200\224 since this is a functional tool, keep most of the UI in the sans-serif for readability.\n- Logo: simple \"Jokdel Royal\" text wordmark in the header, dark navy.\n\n## Part 1: Public Form Site\n\n- **Header**: Jokdel Royal logo, and (if multiple forms exist) a simple nav or dropdown listing available forms/services.\n- **Landing/Home view**: A short welcome section \342\200\224 \"Get in touch with Jokdel Royal\" or similar \342\200\224 followed by a list or grid of available inquiry forms (each shown as a simple card with a title, one-line description, and a \"Fill Form\" button). Since forms are admin-created and can vary in number, this list should render dynamically from whatever forms exist in the admin \342\200\224 do not hardcode exactly 3 forms.\n- **Form view**: Renders whichever form the user selected, built dynamically from the admin\'s field configuration (see Part 2). Standard field types to support: short text, long text/textarea, email, phone number, dropdown/select, radio buttons, checkboxes, number, and date. Include client-side validation (required fields, valid email/phone format). On submit, show a clear confirmation screen (\"Thank you \342\200\224 we\'ll be in touch shortly\") and optionally a WhatsApp quick-contact link as a fallback.\n- **Design**: Centered single-column form layout, generous spacing, dark navy submit button, subtle field focus states in navy, minimal decoration \342\200\224 clean and fast to fill on mobile.\n\n## Part 2: Admin Panel\n\n- **Simple login screen** (email/password \342\200\224 can be a mocked/local-only auth for prototype purposes) leading into a dashboard.\n- **Sidebar navigation** (dark navy background, white text/icons): Dashboard, Forms, Responses/Leads, Settings.\n- **Dashboard**: At-a-glance summary \342\200\224 total forms, total responses, responses this week, and a simple recent-activity list (latest 5\342\200\22310 submissions with form name, submitter name, and timestamp).\n- **Forms section**:\n  - List of all existing forms (table or card list) with form name, number of fields, number of responses, created date, and a status toggle (Active/Inactive \342\200\224 inactive forms don\'t appear on the public site).\n  - **Form Builder**: A drag-and-drop or add/reorder interface to create a new form or edit an existing one. Admin can: set the form title and description, add fields one at a time by choosing a field type (from the types listed above), set each field\'s label, placeholder, whether it\'s required, and options (for dropdown/radio/checkbox fields), reorder fields, and delete fields. Include a live preview panel showing how the form will look to the public as fields are added.\n  - Ability to duplicate an existing form (useful since services/needs vary and staff may want to clone a similar form rather than rebuild from scratch).\n- **Responses/Leads section**:\n  - Table view of all submissions across all forms, with columns: Submitter Name, Contact (email/phone), Form/Service, Date Submitted, Status.\n  - Filter/search by form, date range, or keyword.\n  - **Status pipeline** for simple CRM behavior: each response can be tagged with a status such as \"New,\" \"Contacted,\" \"In Progress,\" \"Closed/Converted,\" \"Not Interested\" \342\200\224 shown as a colored tag (use wine-red for \"New,\" navy or grey tones for others) and changeable via a dropdown directly in the table or in a detail view.\n  - Clicking a response opens a detail view showing all submitted field data for that entry, plus a notes field for staff to log follow-up notes, and the status dropdown.\n  - **Export**: a button to export all responses (or a filtered subset) as a CSV file.\n- **Settings section**: Basic \342\200\224 company contact info shown on the public confirmation screen, WhatsApp number used for the fallback link, and (optional) toggle for email notifications on new submissions.\n\n## Technical Notes\n- Use React with a clean component structure: separate the public site and admin panel as distinct route groups (e.g., `/` for public forms, `/admin` for the CRM).\n- Store forms and responses in local state or a simple mock backend/local storage for the prototype \342\200\224 structure the data model clearly (Form \342\206\222 Fields[], Response \342\206\222 { formId, submittedAt, status, notes, fieldValues{} }) so it could later be wired to a real database.\n- Fully responsive: the public form site must work well on mobile (most inquiries will come from phones); the admin panel should be usable on tablet/desktop, with table views scrolling horizontally on smaller screens if needed.\n- Keep the UI minimal \342\200\224 avoid heavy illustrations, excessive color blocking, or unnecessary animation. This should feel closer to a clean SaaS dashboard than a marketing site. the logo is the uploaded image \n\n\n---"
    items {
      text: "# AI Studio Prompt \342\200\224 Jokdel Royal Form Builder + Simple CRM\n\nPaste this into AI Studio. No images are required for this build, but if you want the public form pages to visually match the main site, you can attach the Home page PNG from earlier for reference.\n\n---\n\nBuild a simple two-part web app for a Nigerian real estate company called **Jokdel Royal** (sales of land & houses, letting/rentals as an intermediary, and property management):\n\n1. A **public-facing form site** where prospective clients fill out inquiry forms.\n2. An **admin panel** where staff can build/edit those forms, view submitted responses, and manage them like a lightweight CRM.\n\nKeep the overall interface **very simple and uncluttered** \342\200\224 this is a lightweight internal tool, not a marketing site. Prioritize clarity and usability over decoration.\n\n## Brand Styling\n- Primary background: white (#FFFFFF).\n- Brand dark navy: #131B2E \342\200\224 headers, nav, primary buttons, form field focus states, admin sidebar background.\n- Accent wine/burgundy: #6E1E1E \342\200\224 used sparingly for status tags, alerts, or \"new\" indicators.\n- Typography: serif (Playfair Display or equivalent) for page titles/headings only; clean sans-serif (Inter or equivalent) for all form labels, inputs, buttons, tables, and body text \342\200\224 since this is a functional tool, keep most of the UI in the sans-serif for readability.\n- Logo: simple \"Jokdel Royal\" text wordmark in the header, dark navy.\n\n## Part 1: Public Form Site\n\n- **Header**: Jokdel Royal logo, and (if multiple forms exist) a simple nav or dropdown listing available forms/services.\n- **Landing/Home view**: A short welcome section \342\200\224 \"Get in touch with Jokdel Royal\" or similar \342\200\224 followed by a list or grid of available inquiry forms (each shown as a simple card with a title, one-line description, and a \"Fill Form\" button). Since forms are admin-created and can vary in number, this list should render dynamically from whatever forms exist in the admin \342\200\224 do not hardcode exactly 3 forms.\n- **Form view**: Renders whichever form the user selected, built dynamically from the admin\'s field configuration (see Part 2). Standard field types to support: short text, long text/textarea, email, phone number, dropdown/select, radio buttons, checkboxes, number, and date. Include client-side validation (required fields, valid email/phone format). On submit, show a clear confirmation screen (\"Thank you \342\200\224 we\'ll be in touch shortly\") and optionally a WhatsApp quick-contact link as a fallback.\n- **Design**: Centered single-column form layout, generous spacing, dark navy submit button, subtle field focus states in navy, minimal decoration \342\200\224 clean and fast to fill on mobile.\n\n## Part 2: Admin Panel\n\n- **Simple login screen** (email/password \342\200\224 can be a mocked/local-only auth for prototype purposes) leading into a dashboard.\n- **Sidebar navigation** (dark navy background, white text/icons): Dashboard, Forms, Responses/Leads, Settings.\n- **Dashboard**: At-a-glance summary \342\200\224 total forms, total responses, responses this week, and a simple recent-activity list (latest 5\342\200\22310 submissions with form name, submitter name, and timestamp).\n- **Forms section**:\n  - List of all existing forms (table or card list) with form name, number of fields, number of responses, created date, and a status toggle (Active/Inactive \342\200\224 inactive forms don\'t appear on the public site).\n  - **Form Builder**: A drag-and-drop or add/reorder interface to create a new form or edit an existing one. Admin can: set the form title and description, add fields one at a time by choosing a field type (from the types listed above), set each field\'s label, placeholder, whether it\'s required, and options (for dropdown/radio/checkbox fields), reorder fields, and delete fields. Include a live preview panel showing how the form will look to the public as fields are added.\n  - Ability to duplicate an existing form (useful since services/needs vary and staff may want to clone a similar form rather than rebuild from scratch).\n- **Responses/Leads section**:\n  - Table view of all submissions across all forms, with columns: Submitter Name, Contact (email/phone), Form/Service, Date Submitted, Status.\n  - Filter/search by form, date range, or keyword.\n  - **Status pipeline** for simple CRM behavior: each response can be tagged with a status such as \"New,\" \"Contacted,\" \"In Progress,\" \"Closed/Converted,\" \"Not Interested\" \342\200\224 shown as a colored tag (use wine-red for \"New,\" navy or grey tones for others) and changeable via a dropdown directly in the table or in a detail view.\n  - Clicking a response opens a detail view showing all submitted field data for that entry, plus a notes field for staff to log follow-up notes, and the status dropdown.\n  - **Export**: a button to export all responses (or a filtered subset) as a CSV file.\n- **Settings section**: Basic \342\200\224 company contact info shown on the public confirmation screen, WhatsApp number used for the fallback link, and (optional) toggle for email notifications on new submissions.\n\n## Technical Notes\n- Use React with a clean component structure: separate the public site and admin panel as distinct route groups (e.g., `/` for public forms, `/admin` for the CRM).\n- Store forms and responses in local state or a simple mock backend/local storage for the prototype \342\200\224 structure the data model clearly (Form \342\206\222 Fields[], Response \342\206\222 { formId, submittedAt, status, notes, fieldValues{} }) so it could later be wired to a real database.\n- Fully responsive: the public form site must work well on mobile (most inquiries will come from phones); the admin panel should be usable on tablet/desktop, with table views scrolling horizontally on smaller screens if needed.\n- Keep the UI minimal \342\200\224 avoid heavy illustrations, excessive color blocking, or unnecessary animation. This should feel closer to a clean SaaS dashboard than a marketing site. the logo is the uploaded image \n\n\n---"
    }
    media {
      mime_type: "image/png"
      blobref {
        blob_id: "/blobstore/prod/makersuite/spanner_managed/global::000054e2ea70026d:0000015f:2:000054e2ea70026d:0000000000000001::b1358ee8c29335ed:00000211240018bc:000658b3d3fadfbf"
      }
      uri: "slazzer-preview-i82kd.png"
